WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.708 --> 00:00:10.483
Each teaching week's activity opens on its own page: the experiment first, then the simulation, then the workbench where you practise the idea.

2
00:00:10.483 --> 00:00:21.488
Make your prediction before anything runs. Commit to one of the predictions, then press run. The gap between your guess and the outcome is where the learning is, so do not skip the commit.

3
00:00:21.488 --> 00:00:32.506
Read the reveal and the mirror. The reveal shows what actually happened; the mirror shows what your prediction says about the lens you carry. Keep one sentence from it in the note box.

4
00:00:32.506 --> 00:00:43.512
Run the simulation more than once. Choose the case, the system, and the safeguard, run one case, then run one hundred. One run contains luck; the batch shows what the system makes more likely. It is a teaching model, not real population numbers.

5
00:00:43.512 --> 00:00:54.519
Practise in the workbench. Every choice gives instant feedback: match examples to mechanisms, make decisions, flip defaults, assemble a system, or weigh policy levers. Week 5's activity is a bias audit that re-enacts Buolamwini and Gebru's Gender Shades study, and it can save your audit as a Word file.

6
00:00:54.519 --> 00:01:05.529
Write the activity note before you leave. One sentence about what the activity helped you notice. It lands in Generate Your Weekly Notes when you return to the week.

7
00:01:05.529 --> 00:01:11.646
The activity is practice. Nothing is scored, recorded, or visible to your professor.

8
00:01:11.646 --> 00:01:17.384
Where next: Go back to the week page for the reflection and your weekly notes.
